"""Author additive MADGER conflict canon; preserve source character identities."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,copy
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
BASE=R.parent/'v3.0.0' if (R.parent/'v3.0.0/World_Atlas.json').exists() else R.parent/'world-depth/source'
W=json.loads((BASE/'World_Atlas.json').read_text());B=json.loads((BASE/'Blueprint_Data.json').read_text())
C={c['id']:c for c in W['characters']};(R/'output').mkdir(exist_ok=True)
D={'edition':'3.2.0','title':'MADGER - The Cost of Control','date':'2026-10-21','base_commit':'a31a6f32286a198f4da0d95b4a1b7f0282c830fc','scope':'Six sources of pressure within the existing factions; thirteen character addenda; seven reusable locations; a season-one evidence and escalation ledger; two complete antagonist stories.','status_rule':'Operating histories, location aliases and fixed normal-state details are additive writer canon. Character choices, scene seeds and complete stories are authored options until adopted by a dated episode ledger. No pilot villain reveal is added.','moral':'A good act does not buy ownership of another person. Protection earns trust when people can question it, refuse it and help shape it.','base_factions':copy.deepcopy(W['factions']),'pressure_centers':[],'character_addenda':[],'locations':[]}
def pressure(id,title,faction,members,place,promise,history,routine,conflict,limits,turn,hero):
 D['pressure_centers'].append(dict(id=id,title=title,faction_id=faction,members=members,member_names=[C[x]['name'] for x in members],place_id=place,promise=promise,history=history,routine=routine,internal_conflict=conflict,limits=limits,story_turn=turn,hero_test=hero))
pressure('PC-01','Gilt Ledger: the price of certainty','FAC-04',['VIL-01','VIL-02','VIL-03'],'EXT-01-B03',
'You will never have to wonder whether the next wagon is coming.',
'Veyra built her influence by solving real supply problems. During the flood seven years before the pilot, she paid for the second rescue convoy without a public bargain. That choice saved people. In the years afterward, she acquired depot contracts and began treating successful provision as evidence that communities should surrender future choice. Her later coercion does not retroactively make the rescue a staged crime.',
'Bale opens with a physical stock count. Corvin arrives later with amendments that turn ordinary purchases into longer obligations. Workers load legitimate orders beside selectively withheld freight. Some admire Veyra because she pays consistently; others have families waiting outside her terms. The depot should feel attractive and competent before the cost of that competence becomes visible.',
'Veyra wants a defensible system. Corvin needs private exceptions that make him indispensable. Bale needs a workable loading floor. A side commission can enrich Corvin while making both the policy and the stock count harder to defend. They cooperate because each supplies something the others lack, not because they share one conscience.',
'The Ledger owns particular stock and businesses. It does not own Reedbank, the public kitchen, the Burrow gates, every road or every merchant. Glass Thread can refuse its jobs. Riven Claw can raid its freight. A supply promise remains limited by wagons, labor and weather.',
'When the rescue invoice becomes public, the apparent twist is that Veyra truly helped. The deeper conflict is whether gratitude can be converted into consent. Let her make one costly good choice in the present while retaining an unacceptable demand elsewhere.',
'Madger must acknowledge the rescue honestly and still let residents reject the monopoly. Pell makes the terms understandable; Iona tests whether the promised supply can actually be delivered.')
pressure('PC-02','Glass Thread: the right fact in the wrong hands','FAC-05',['VIL-04','VIL-05','VIL-06'],'EXT-03-U04',
'If they will not record us, we will make their records impossible to ignore.',
'Nix returned from teenage work seasons with Madger to a Millmere restoration process that overlooked some households. Their grievance is genuine. Later paid access work gave them equipment, contacts and a dangerous audience. Tessera joined for profitable anonymity, and Patch followed maintenance pay. Their shared origin story is convenient: each remembers a different moment when the work stopped being defensible.',
'They lease rooms briefly, pay for ordinary meals, mend equipment and argue over what a client actually purchased. Tessera separates identities; Nix keeps recognizable scraps of old route language; Patch labels components because a wrong part still breaks a machine. The crew has no universal view of the world. An unattended message is as likely to remain unread as anyone else’s.',
'Nix wants the public to know who forced the truth into view. Tessera wants nobody to connect a useful identity to a crime. Patch wants a future workshop whose tools are not evidence. Each can expose the other two, but doing so would also expose their own choices.',
'D17 is a bounded receipt replay through a compromised trade relay. No code, credentials or real intrusion procedure is specified. Corrupted messages do not open a physical latch. Glass Thread needs a particular access opportunity and can lose it through ordinary witnesses and source comparison.',
'A surviving document proves that an omitted community really existed, while a different document proves that Nix endangered travelers. Both stay true. The heroes cannot dismiss the grievance because its messenger committed a crime, or excuse the crime because the grievance is true.',
'Bot must admit what a record cannot establish. Madger must rescue strangers before pursuing an old friend. Snow follows ordinary scent and behavior; she cannot identify a hacker or detect guilt.')
pressure('PC-03','Riven Claw: the cost of spectacle','FAC-06',['VIL-07','VIL-08','VIL-09'],'SITE-07',
'Nobody will leave us hungry or treat us as disposable again.',
'The Cinder freight diversion eighteen years before the pilot caused real hardship. Brakka, once a convoy guard, founded her band seven years ago. Those dates are distinct: she did not invent the decline and has no mandate from the people harmed by it. The occupied civilian terraces remain a community of workers and families, most of whom want the raiders gone.',
'Dreg sorts freight, checks harnesses and apportions meals. Vessa reports routes she has actually observed. Brakka stages arrivals and punishments as theater. A ledger of shares keeps the band together as much as fear does. Rest, damaged wheels and insufficient water can postpone a raid; animals and workers cannot march indefinitely for a dramatic cut.',
'Brakka wants obedience visible from a distance. Dreg wants usable provisions. Vessa wants her knowledge respected and an escape from expendability. Burning supplies can therefore satisfy the leader while destroying the bargain that keeps her specialists working.',
'The band travels by known routes. Vessa’s flight has weather and load limits. It cannot appear inside the Burrow or bypass inner defensive closure. Ashmouth is separate from civilian Cinder Cuts. Trade with Serik or a Ledger intermediary creates a transaction, not a chain of command.',
'Dreg’s record of victims first looks like conscience. It began as insurance for himself. What changes him is not discovering a hidden tender nature, but choosing to use a useful bargaining asset for restitution and accepting what that costs.',
'Gorilla must protect a retreat instead of accepting a public challenge. Madger must work within the town’s assigned roles. Edda treats injured people without confusing treatment with exemption from responsibility.')
pressure('PC-04','Salt Crown: scarcity made proprietary','FAC-04',['NEW-08'],'EXT-08-B03',
'One coordinated water account is fairer than a thousand arguments at the wells.',
'Kavar acquired pump interests through successive dry seasons. His certificates began as convenient records of delivered water and became instruments of dependence. He is a commercial counterpart within the Ledger network, not a new global faction or Veyra’s puppet. Ossara’s three public wells predate his business by centuries.',
'Public-facing clerks weigh claims, inspect containers and explain legitimate maintenance costs. Kavar surrounds the process with handsome objects and precise language. The lie sits in a specific maintenance account and a competing pump kept closed, not in the existence of arithmetic or every clerk’s intentions.',
'Local staff need their wages and reliable water for their own homes. Some believe closure protects an exhausted aquifer. Kavar benefits from their caution while withholding the evidence that would let them distinguish a real limit from a manufactured one.',
'Exposing a false debt does not refill the aquifer. Pumps still need parts, trained maintainers and water-budget decisions. The counting house has records and instruments, not a hidden control that governs every well in the desert.',
'The first exposed invoice is genuinely wrong, but a second shortage is real. A cheap victory would accuse every warning of being a lie. A strong episode makes Maela defend a measured restriction moments after helping expose Kavar’s fabricated one.',
'The heroes must accept local expertise. Madger cannot promise limitless water; Commonworks can help restore equipment only under a community-agreed allocation.')
pressure('PC-05','Blackwake Sails: rescue with a ransom attached','FAC-08',['NEW-09'],'SITE-05',
'When everyone else turns back, we are the ones who come for you.',
'Serik was a capable rescue captain before he began turning emergency passage into ransom. Expulsion did not erase his skill. He now commands three small cargo craft and uses an abandoned mooring outside Aurel Quay. Former clients remember real rescues, making testimony against him emotionally difficult without making it unreliable.',
'Crews mend sails, calculate loads and argue about shares. Serik rehearses the arrival he wants survivors to remember. His people know that a generous-looking rescue can become a debt at the next berth. Some remain for money, others because leaving requires a safe port and a captain willing to hire them.',
'Serik needs admiration; captains need craft that can return. A spectacular pursuit can cost a vessel and turn supporters into witnesses. Eri’s knowledge of ordinary load rules is more useful than discovering a mystical weakness.',
'Three craft remain three craft. He cannot make storms, control the liftstone field or command every sky crew. Heavy cargo, wind and counterweights constrain him. The mooring has three docking arms and two refuge cells, not an unbounded fleet base.',
'He saves a crew member during the escape because he has real rescue instincts. The same survivor can later testify that he held others for ransom. A good act complicates the portrait; it does not cancel the charge.',
'Local navigators lead. Gorilla protects loading space, Bot checks stated load data and Madger helps free people without promising an impossible airlift.')
pressure('PC-06','Still Bell: authority after the emergency','FAC-09',['NEW-10','NEW-05'],'SITE-06',
'We remember what happens when a confident person reconnects the wrong thing.',
'The Severance occurred ninety-two years before the pilot. Ossa did not cause it. She inherited accounts of a real protective sacrifice and later forged one modern prohibition to retain control over decisions. Most archivists preserve useful knowledge; the institution contains both protective work and authoritarian choices.',
'Archive staff compare copies, inspect material and send field teams when weather allows. Genuine hazard reports share shelves with Ossa’s altered order. Ysolde’s disclosure work requires painstaking comparison, not an eloquent accusation followed by instant certainty.',
'Ossa fears that admitting one forgery will make people ignore every warning. Ysolde fears that secrecy will eventually make every warning unbelievable. Ysolde must publish her own family’s benefit from prolonged closure rather than placing herself outside the history she reveals.',
'The Cut Span retains a real gap and resonance hazard. The forged modern order and the justified old closure are separate claims. Exposing the first does not make the second safe. Echo ice stores fragments, not omniscient testimony or resurrected witnesses.',
'The dramatic victory is a shared process that can test both claims, preserving one closure while withdrawing another. Ossa loses exclusive judgment, not the usefulness of everything she learned.',
'Bot keeps uncertainty visible; Commonworks tests physical claims. Madger learns to live with a partial reopening that serves people without pretending the whole ancient network is safe.')

# Character addenda preserve every baseline card, likeness, age, relationship and voice status.
def arc(id,private,habit,pressure,choice,cost,next_scene):
 D['character_addenda'].append({'id':id,'name':C[id]['name'],'baseline':copy.deepcopy(C[id]),'private_history':private,'ordinary_habit':habit,'pressure_point':pressure,'irreversible_choice':choice,'cost_of_change':cost,'scene_seed':next_scene,'status':'Past detail and habitual behavior are additive writer canon. The choice and scene are proposed dramatic development, not a completed dated event.'})
arc('VIL-01','As a young depot worker, Veyra once stayed after closing to match an unclaimed parcel to the family whose name had been copied incorrectly. She remembers the correction as proof that a reliable system needs someone willing to take charge.','Straightens the unused chair before a negotiation, even when she expects the other person to refuse it.','A public subscription solves a supply problem she sincerely believed required her contract.','Releases a finite emergency allotment before anyone signs her preferred agreement.','She loses a real bargaining advantage. Workers and residents may acknowledge the release while still opposing the monopoly.','Madger thanks her in public. She waits for the second sentence that will turn thanks into consent. It does not come.')
arc('VIL-02','Corvin’s first profitable introduction united two traders who later stopped inviting him once they knew each other. He has spent years designing relationships that seem unable to continue without him.','Remembers a clerk’s favorite tea and forgets the clerk’s complaint about unpaid hours.','Two clients compare the same amendment without his explanation in the room.','Signs an account naming his own side fee instead of blaming a junior clerk.','He loses access to a useful table; telling one truth does not make his other contracts honest.','He arrives early to a feast and discovers his reserved seat has been given to someone who performed the work.')
arc('VIL-03','Bale learned during winter shortages that a count can be numerically correct while a household is missing from it. He later chose to prize balanced shelves over inconvenient recipients.','Carries a plain pencil worn almost too short to hold; he dislikes wasting useful material.','Edda asks him to say the names attached to a shipment he has called merely delayed.','Releases the withheld allotment and preserves the signed instruction that implicated him.','Dismissal, testimony and restitution follow; stock movement alone does not repair the harm.','A former neighbor recognizes his handwriting on both the old request and the new refusal.')
arc('VIL-04','Nix kept an unanswered copy of a restoration appeal beside an early joking letter from Madger. The documents record two different losses: public exclusion and a friendship allowed to fade. Neither proves that Madger deliberately abandoned them.','Repairs loose handles in rooms they are about to leave, then insists they do not care what happens there.','An authentic omitted-household list is found beside a knowingly false travel instruction.','Returns a piece of leverage that could have bought escape, allowing a specific endangered traveler to be found.','The immediate act of help cannot restore friendship. Later testimony and restrictions remain necessary.','Madger remembers the joke at the top of the old letter. Nix wants that recognition to settle an argument it cannot settle.')
arc('VIL-05','Tessera learned as a dispatch assistant that polished manners made some visitors more believable than tired workers with accurate information. She chose to profit from that prejudice.','Leaves a borrowed room cleaner than she found it; an ordinary kindness becomes a witness’s memorable detail.','A witness identifies a repeated behavior that does not depend on her chosen name.','Burns a profitable identity to escape a narrowing inquiry rather than forcing Patch to take the whole blame.','She loses access she cannot instantly replace. The gesture is self-preservation mixed with attachment, not a completed redemption.','A witness remembers her repairing a loose parcel strap. The fact identifies a moment of presence, not every crime she committed.')
arc('VIL-06','Patch’s first repair for Nix was lawful. He kept the payment note as the imagined first entry in his future workshop ledger, then continued accepting jobs after the purpose changed.','Labels broken parts with the actual fault rather than the owner’s preferred excuse.','The component he could destroy is both incriminating evidence and the only way to reconstruct an injury-causing message.','Stops the harmful maintenance job and preserves the component and its context for investigators.','He loses income, risks prosecution and must identify earlier work. Bot offers a fair hearing, not secret immunity.','A child admires the neatness of his repair. Patch has to decide what accomplishment he is willing to explain.')
arc('VIL-07','Brakka’s famous first victory began with a frightened decision to hold a narrow loading space until help arrived. She later retold it as proof that she had never been afraid.','Checks everyone else’s portions while leaving her own plate untouched until she has an audience.','Her crew saves useful cargo instead of obeying an order meant only to humiliate opponents.','Chooses to let a subordinate retreat without treating the retreat as betrayal, or openly doubles down and loses them.','Either branch changes her actual support. Fear cannot be restored by one louder speech.','A witness describes the first victory truthfully and with respect. Brakka hears the admission of fear as an insult.')
arc('VIL-08','Dreg began recording victims to protect himself in a future dispute over shares. Over time, individual names made it harder to call the stolen freight ownerless.','Repairs a frayed harness before counting the goods carried by it.','His relatives reject food they know was taken from another household.','Uses his private victim record to return property even when the record also exposes his own share.','His family can remain angry. His crew can refuse him. Restitution is a continuing obligation.','A crate contains the replacement part for a village pump; selling it by weight would make the ledger profitable and the village poorer.')
arc('VIL-09','Vessa once sold the same safe-weather estimate to two crews that could not both occupy the ridge shelter. She told herself she had sold information, not made a promise.','Tests a knot by touch while talking, often noticing its weakness before she admits the weakness of an agreement.','Bad wind forces her to walk beside travelers who know her scouting work endangered them.','Discloses previously sold routes before accepting Eri’s offer of legitimate guiding work.','An employer may still decline her. Flight and a warning do not earn a blank reputation.','Two honest clients ask which one she will guide when the weather leaves room for only one journey.')
arc('NEW-08','Kavar once improved a leaking pump cheaply and earned real gratitude. He now retells the repair as evidence that public access exists only because he permits it.','Polishes the display measure while allowing an inconvenient maintenance report to remain unread.','A repaired public pump reduces the need for his certificates, but the aquifer still requires limits.','Surrenders withheld equipment with its accurate service history.','He loses an exclusive advantage and must face the falsified debt separately.','Maela defends a genuine water restriction on the same afternoon she proves his account was false.')
arc('NEW-09','Serik kept the names of people from his early rescues. He later began counting captives in the same private book, preserving the flattering heading.','Inspects a stranger’s poorly tied cargo knot before delivering an elegant threat.','A crew member repeats his own rescue rule while refusing a profitable overloaded departure.','Abandons ransom cargo to save a crew member, then faces testimony from the people he would have sold.','A lost craft or contract narrows his reach; skill remains dangerous without giving him supernatural recovery.','A survivor thanks him for one rescue and names him for another extortion in the same hearing.')
arc('NEW-10','Ossa’s parent was born after the Severance and inherited the accounts rather than witnessing the cuts. Ossa’s certainty is therefore an institutional inheritance, not a personal memory of ninety-two years ago.','Touches the edge of a copied seal before reading the words, as though familiarity could establish authenticity.','A young archivist asks why one genuine hazardous closure needs a forged companion order.','Releases the altered order and accepts independent review while continuing to defend the measured real hazard.','Her judgment can still be useful without remaining final. She may lose office and the deference attached to it.','During a safe inspection, she correctly stops a careless action. The person who obeys her still asks to see the measurements afterward.')
arc('IND-01','Pip’s first stall sold repaired household goods with owners’ names still attached. Removing inconvenient names gradually became part of the service they offered.','Rewraps a fragile object beautifully, sometimes while avoiding the question of how it arrived.','Two plausible owners tell incompatible stories; a purchase receipt proves a sale but not rightful possession.','Returns a valuable item and names the supplier without buying a promise of forgiveness.','Criminal customers leave before lawful customers trust them. Cheerfulness does not shorten that interval.','Pell recognizes a label from Pip’s first honest stall on the back of an object that should never have been sold.')

